{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209992,"nonce":"k+Z9bnk5sHVBBEguMXXpm3986sg=","title":"**What is wrong.** Running `golangci-lint run ./...` through the local shell","message":"wrapper mis-parses `run` as a path argument. It prints \"No issues found\" and\nexits non-zero, so the output says the tree is clean while the command has\nactually failed. `rtk proxy golangci-lint run ./...` behaves correctly. CI is\nunaffected — `.github/workflows/ci.yml` invokes the binary directly.\n\n**Why it matters.** `golangci-lint` is where the depguard layering rules live, and\nthose rules are the only mechanical enforcement of the module boundaries\n`CONTEXT.md` §5 describes. An agent or contributor who runs the documented\ncommand locally gets a green that means nothing and can commit a layering\nviolation believing they checked. `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:26-28` makes \"golangci-lint\nmust pass before an agent reports done\" a build rule, so this quietly voids one of\nthe three standing gates.\n\n**Where.** The local shell wrapper (not in this repo), `Makefile` `lint` target,\n`justfile`, `README.md:50`, `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:26-28`.\n\n**Done when.** The documented local command either works or fails loudly, and\n`README.md` / `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md` name the working invocation. A wrapper that\ncannot be fixed should be worked around in the Makefile target so that `make lint`\nis trustworthy, since that is what the docs tell people to run.","files":null}]}